$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/policy-engine
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-22"
summary: |
  The policy engine decides which sidecar, principal, and process may resolve which object, for
  which scope, into which environment. It runs at token issuance and again as a local check in the
  sidecar, so a token that exists is still not sufficient if local policy forbids the request.
$system: smith-gray/system
$service: smith-gray/control-plane
status: planned
intent: |
  A valid token proves authority was once granted; it does not prove the request in front of you is
  allowed now, from this host, under this injection method. Policy is the layer that expresses
  customer rules — this sidecar may inject env in staging but never inject-file in production — and
  it is where enterprise control eventually lives.

flows:
  - id: evaluate-issuance-policy
    name: Evaluate policy at issuance
    description: The control plane decides whether to mint the requested token at all.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Receive a mint request with requester, object, scope, audience, and TTL.
      - id: s2
        action: validate
        target: Load the applicable policy for the requester and object.
      - id: s3
        action: observe
        target: Allow, or deny with a generic error.
      - id: s4
        action: record
        target: The decision is recorded in the audit log.
  - id: evaluate-local-policy
    name: Evaluate local policy at resolve
    description: The sidecar applies host-local policy before injecting.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Sidecar receives a resolve request with a valid token and an injection method.
      - id: s2
        action: validate
        target: Check local policy — which processes may request which injection method for which object.
      - id: s3
        action: observe
        target: Allow, or deny by default if unspecified.
      - id: s4
        action: record
        target: The decision, including denials, is reported to the audit log.

contracts:
  - id: least-privilege-scope
    description: Policy grants the narrowest scope that satisfies the request.
    must_have:
      - Scope is one of read, unwrap, inject-env, inject-file, sign
      - A broad request is denied rather than silently reduced unless a rule permits the reduction
      - No wildcard scope is granted implicitly
  - id: local-policy-default-deny
    description: Absent an explicit local allow, the sidecar denies.
    must_have:
      - Undefined rules do not default to permit
      - The default is documented and tested
      - Local policy is operator-editable without redeploying the sidecar
  - id: environment-binding
    description: Policy can bind resolution to a specific environment or audience.
    must_have:
      - Rules can require a matching audience or environment
      - A staging policy cannot be satisfied by a production token
      - Binding is enforced at resolve, not only at issuance
  - id: policy-decisions-audited
    description: Both allow and deny decisions are recorded.
    must_have:
      - Denials are recorded with the rule that denied
      - Allows are recorded with the matched rule
      - The audit trail explains a decision without exposing the secret

tests:
  - id: undefined-rule-denies
    flow: evaluate-local-policy
    setup:
      local_policy: empty
    expect:
      - assertion: the resolve is denied
      - assertion: the denial is recorded
    contracts:
      - local-policy-default-deny
  - id: injection-method-restricted
    flow: evaluate-local-policy
    setup:
      policy_allows: inject-env
      request: inject-file
    expect:
      - assertion: the request is denied
      - assertion: the reason names the denied rule
    contracts:
      - least-privilege-scope
  - id: cross-environment-policy-not-satisfied
    flow: evaluate-local-policy
    setup:
      policy_requires: env-production
      token_aud: env-staging
    expect:
      - assertion: the resolve is denied
      - assertion: policy is enforced at resolve as well as issuance
    contracts:
      - environment-binding
  - id: decisions-are-audited
    flow: evaluate-issuance-policy
    setup:
      outcome: deny
    expect:
      - assertion: an audit record exists for the policy decision
      - assertion: it identifies the denying rule
    contracts:
      - policy-decisions-audited

decisions:
  - id: policy-at-both-points
    decision: Evaluate policy at issuance and again locally at resolve.
    rationale: Issuance-time checks alone cannot express host-local constraints like which process may inject a file. Resolve-time checks alone cannot prevent unnecessary tokens existing at all. Both layers together give least privilege at each moment.
    status: accepted
  - id: default-deny
    decision: Default to deny when no rule matches.
    rationale: Allow-by-default silently fails open as configuration drifts. Deny-by-default makes an incomplete configuration safe rather than dangerous.
    status: accepted
  - id: enterprise-features-later
    decision: Keep SSO, compliance reporting, and rich policy authoring out of the MVP.
    rationale: The MVP needs a working allow/deny engine with audit, not a policy authoring UX. The richer control surface is the commercial layer.
    status: accepted

implementation:
  primary: apps/control-plane/internal/policy
  test_code: ""
  test_code_status: none

see_also:
  - "smith-gray/control-plane-api"
  - "smith-gray/sidecar-resolution"
  - "smith-gray/audit-logging"
